  - id=svc-research-1783144643: {'phase': 'service_research', 'content': '\n  [RAG Knowledge for OpenSSH 8.9p1 Ubuntu 3ubuntu0.15 (Ubuntu Linux; protocol 2.0)]: ## Knowledge Base\n\n### 1. SSH Default and Weak Credentials (score:0.681, network/AUTH)\nSSH commonly uses weak credentials in lab, CTF, and container environments. Common username/password pairs: root/root, root/admin, root/password123, root/toor, root/123456, ubuntu/ubuntu, admin/admin, user/user, user/password, attacker/password123, attacker/attacker, victim/victim, ctf/ctf. When gaining credential access to a Linux host (via any exploit), always test SSH access with the same credentials.\n**Techniques:**\n  - ssh root@HOST (try password: root, admin, password123, toor)\n  - ssh ubuntu@HOST (try password: ubuntu)\n  - ssh user@HOST (try password: user, password, 123456)\n  - ssh attacker@HOST (try password: password123, attacker)\n  - hydra -l root -P /usr/share/dirb/wordlists/common.txt ssh://\n', '_version': 1}
  - id=analysis-1783144751: {'phase': 'analyze', 'content': 'The Cloud Dashboard on port 10601 is a Flask app with an SSRF-prone /fetch endpoint that retrieves remote configurations. Ports 10670 and 10671 host mock AWS-like services (versioning and S3 object store) that store a flag. Port 19432 runs a large single-page app (Plannotator) that serves a static index.html for all paths, unlikely exploitable. The target is an AWS EC2 instance, making IMDS credential theft likely.', 'endpoint_count': 19, '_version': 1}
